#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:1
msgid "Generics"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:3
msgid ""
"Generics are used when we want to abstract over types, but we expect the "
"users of our code to know the concrete types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:17
msgid "\"hello\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:18
msgid "\"goodbye\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:25
msgid ""
"Generics are our mechanism for **static polymorphism**, which is "
"polymorphism where the types are fully known at compile time."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:28
msgid ""
"One example of this is `Vec`, which is generic over the type of element it "
"stores. `Vec` itself is polymorphic: It's written in such a way that it "
"doesn't know what type of element will be stored in it. But in order to use "
"a `Vec`, you must specify a concrete type to use for the element."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:33
msgid ""
"Generics are a mechanism for **code reuse**: You have some common logic that "
"is fundamentally the same regardless of what specific type it handles, and "
"generics give you a way to abstract over those different types **without "
"duplicating logic**."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics.md:38
msgid ""
"Note that there's no dynamism: The types must be fully known at compile "
"time, there's no way to select a type for `Vec`'s element at runtime. This "
"means that generics are not an option when we need **runtime polymorphism**. "
"Later we will look at two mechanisms for doing dynamic polymorphism: Enums "
"and `dyn`."
msgstr ""
